# Portfolio guardrails

This is a personal Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind portfolio. Keep `app/`, `components/`, `data.ts`, and the existing visual design unless the user explicitly asks to change them.

## Before editing

State the plan in chat first: which files you will add/change/delete, and why. Wait for the user to proceed if the plan is more than a small, local tweak. Prefer the smallest change that solves the request. Do not refactor, restyle, or reorganize adjacent code.

## TypeScript

Keep TypeScript strict. Do not weaken types. Never use `any`, `as any`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, or non-null assertions (`!`) unless the user explicitly approves that escape.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — type escape
const items = data as any;

// ✅ GOOD — typed, matches existing patterns
import { projects } from "../data";
```

## Forbidden without explicit approval

Do not do any of the following unless the user clearly asks:

- Upgrade or add dependencies; run `npm audit fix`; edit `package.json` or lockfiles
- Add paid services, API keys, analytics, databases, or environment variables
- Commit, push, create branches, open PRs, or merge

Existing `@vercel/analytics` / Speed Insights stay as-is; do not add more telemetry.

## Verify changes

After code edits, tell the user how to check them:

- UI/layout: `npm run dev`, then open the affected page and exercise the change
- Production/type safety: `npm run build`
- Optional: `npm run lint`

Do not start long-running servers unless asked. If you cannot verify in the browser, say so.
